  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nThere will be somewhat of a lull in rain chances heading into \nWednesday early evening before another round of widespread \nshowers and thunderstorm move through overnight into Thursday \nmorning. \n\nThe area of upper ascent that brings the first round will move east \ninto the Ohio Valley, as the second is almost a synoptic repeat of \nthe first. The difference here is in the mid to upper pattern, which \nbuckles slightly back to the north under the influence of the very \nslight eastward shift in the upper low over the Rockies. Between the \ntwo waves, isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms remain \npossible along the meandering surface boundary. By late Wednesday \nevening into Wednesday night, mid/upper vorticity leans eastward \nwith another boost in upper ascent. \n\nWhat has become more clear is the long range solutions in the \nevolution of the upper low, which have come into better alignment. \nThis concerns the late Wednesday into Thursday morning timeframe, \nwhen both ECM/GFS global guidance now show the upper low being \nsheared out, spreading it between an amplifying Canadian trough, and \nretrograding its southern fringes back to the southwest CONUS. The \nimpact here is for better support to be pulled slightly northwest \ninto NE, IA, and northern MO. By Thursday afternoon, the system \nejects into northern IL and southern WI, pulling a stronger cold \nfront southeastward. Much of the CWA remains in the warm sector, \nthough broken/overcast skies will limit surface instability, \nespecially north of I-70. With this in mind, as the upper support \ncontinues to move east-northeast into the Great Lakes Region, the \npotential for showers and thunderstorms will rely largely on frontal \nconvergence, becoming more scattered than widespread Thursday \nafternoon and evening. One positive with this is that locally heavy \nrain, resulting from thunderstorms, will shift between the two \nprimary episodes of higher rain chances. LPMM does have spots of 3 \ninches, but the fact that it comes in two rounds, varies in \nlocation, and has good eastward movement, should mitigate flood \npotential. Also consider that is has recently been very dry and river \nlevels are running lower with the recent dry stretch. I wouldn't \nexpect much more than nuisance ponding under the heaviest rainfall.\n\nBy Thursday night, the front clears the area and brings much cooler, \nbelow normal temperatures to the region. Long range guidance seems \nto be making a compromise between cool temperatures and freezing \ntemperatures once advertised by the GFS. Dry conditions persist as \nhigh pressure building in from the northern Plains and crosses the \narea west to east between Friday and Sunday. Highs will be in the \n60s Friday and Saturday. The coolest temperatures come Saturday \nmorning with the surface high overhead. Lows in urban areas settle \nin the low-40s with outlying areas possibly as cool as the mid to \nupper 30s.",
